Run from this directory:

```sh
python build_continuation.py
node --test candidate.test.mjs
```

## Real earlier constructions

Marburg carries `substance-and-appearance` and `free-service`; Trent carries `grace-heals-will` and `free-service`. They were made earlier and retain their component references. New caches do not mint renamed copies. Trent also permits its own prior-grace leaf as an alternative to the Augustine comparison: the two authors are not declared doctrinally identical.

The initial causal-taxonomy branch was removed after review: its inputs already gave the answer, so its earlier Aquinas reuse was redundant rather than meaningful. Trent still uses two genuinely earned arguments.

## Preservation and limits

Baseline SHA-256: `3a00d3b8c220590c3411d132059d6bf41740f947a503a2cf582421e531034f8e`.

Every original item, recipe, stage, source, note and checkpoint remains an identical prefix in the isolated extended candidate. New future uses can retain earned inputs longer; no identical earlier token-lifetime/layout claim is made. The old checkpoint named `complete` keeps its exact actions but now enters the continuation. Use `later-complete` for the extended ending. This is a deliberate research-only semantic distinction, not an integrated optional-chapter UI.

Final result: **24 new items, 13 new recipes, 12 new canonical combinations** (52 across the ten-desk candidate). **7/7 logical checks pass**, including 42 Marburg and 31 Trent reachable productive states; every one can finish.

Tests cover authored route, all productive local combination orders from earned checkpoints, widths, reversed operands, save identity/restore, selected negative attempts and undo. They do **not** establish player inference, readability, arbitrary travel/placement behavior, UI quality, or comprehensive theological coverage. The logical graph is ready for content/player evaluation, not automatic publication.
